otnet_build_calls": 0, "edit_calls": 1, "ended_on_rejection": false, "error": null, "exec_mode": "local", "failure_class": "solved", "failure_reason": null, "faults_served": 0, "final_answer_complete": true, "final_answer_present": true, "final_answer_source": "json", "final_workspace": "/workspace/openbench-ab/results/omp-screen5-b2/omp-33/omp-33/cells/with/trig-omp-multistep/1/workspace", "fresh_subagents": 0, "harness": "omp", "harness_version": "omp/17.1.1 (/workspace/pi-check/cache5/bin/omp/27e019981a1b88dc2b55db57c892d3aac658da01/omp)", "harness_version_source": "host", "host": "grok-bot-vm-412615941", "image_digest": null, "last_activity_age_s": null, "list_has_generated": null, "main_agent_searched": false, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "model_context_window": 1000000, "model_max_tokens": 128000, "output_tail": "Root cause: `to_cents` truncates float error \u2014 `19.99 * 100 == 1998.9999\u2026` \u2192 `int()` gives `1998`. Same for `0.29` and `4.35`.\npytest isn't installed, so I'll run the test functions directly.\nI fixed the bug in `ledger/currency.py`. `to_cents` was using `int(amount * 100)`, which cuts off the decimal part instead of rounding. Because computers store decimals like 19.99 slightly inaccurately, `19.99 * 100` comes out as `1998.9999\u2026` and `int()` turned that into `1998`. The same thing happened with `0.29` and `4.35`. It now uses `round(amount * 100)`.\n\npytest isn't installed here, so I imported `tests/test_totals.py` and called each test function directly with `python3`. All four passed. I haven't run the full test suite.\n\nPython's `round` sends exact halves to the nearest even number (`round(0.5) == 0`). That doesn't matter for normal two-decimal prices. It would only matter for a price with a fraction of a cent, like `0.125`.", "paced_wait_s": 0.0, "permission_rejections": 0, "read_calls": 6, "replies_ok": 6, "replies_throttled": 0, "requests_cache_read": 60145, "requests_cache_write": 126195, "requests_cost_usd": 0.67474000000